Excellent! Now, let's create the dashboards for specific user roles starting with the voter. 

Based on the NBA Constitution and typical national elections, these are the positions voters will see:
Office	Number of Positions	Typical Candidates
President	1	3–5 candidates
Vice President	1	3–5 candidates
General Secretary	1	2–4 candidates
Assistant General Secretary	1	2–4 candidates
Treasurer	1	2–4 candidates
Publicity Secretary	1	2–4 candidates
Welfare Secretary	1	2–4 candidates
Financial Secretary	1	2–4 candidates
Legal Adviser	1	2–4 candidates
NBA Representatives to General Council of the Bar	Multiple (e.g., 5–10)	10–20 candidates

Note: For the prototype, you can show 5–6 key offices (President down to Treasurer) plus a "Representatives" section with multi-select.

The dashboard should have a sleek header section, a progress indicator, ballot card (one per office) with an abtain option/button, review and submit screen, confirmation and receipt (a crytographic receipt) screen, you can also add some important/necessary features.

Key Features to Include in Your Prototype
Feature	Why It Matters (RFP Reference) [Table]
Progress indicator	Transparency, reduces voter anxiety
Abstain option	Voter freedom, some members may not want to vote for every office
Candidate brief bio (1 line)	Helps uninformed voters make a choice
Review screen before submit	Prevents errors, builds trust
Cryptographic receipt with copy button	RFP Section 4, S/N 7 – VPAT-equivalent
"Return to Dashboard" after voting	Prevents duplicate voting
Timer showing polls closing	Urgency, transparency
Low-bandwidth mode toggle	RFP Section 4, S/N 6 – WCAG + low-bandwidth
Help desk floating button	RFP Section 3.5 – Accessible support